Run the Imp Swarm

Start with one parent state:

I_0=(X,\;PB,\;M)

where X is the thing we’re exploring, PB the Pattern Book, and M the current Metamap.

At every semantic junction:

J=\{j_1,j_2,\ldots,j_n\}

fork an imp:

I_0\rightarrow\{I_1,I_2,\ldots,I_n\}.

But these are symbolic/latent copies, not independent agents actually running somewhere. Each inherits the same starting Pattern Book and intent, then takes a different track.

The instruction is deliberately tiny:

Go to your junction. Mark it. Explore it. Bring back one souvenir.

No definition of souvenir.

That’s important.

One imp may return with an invariant. Another with a contradiction. Another with an equation. Another with a pun. Another with a new category. Another with a failed route. Another with a question. Another with a transform. Another with an affordance. Another with something that doesn’t fit any existing type.

That last case is especially valuable:

\text{souvenir}\notin\mathcal C_{\text{known}}

becomes an O-IOE trigger:

\boxed{\text{Do we need a new category to store what came back?}}

Each imp marks the junction

The marker should contain:

m_i=
(
\text{origin},
